from .common import *
from .lg import S


def R(src, snk, bend=0.45):
    return A(src, snk, bend, rad=True)


def theory(B):
    B.h2("13.2  Dissolving-metal reductions: regiochemistry and scope")
    B.ul(["<b>Birch reduction rules.</b> Arene + Na (or Li)/NH<sub>3</sub>(l) with an alcohol gives a 1,4-cyclohexadiene. Electron-<i>donating</i> substituents (alkyl, OR, NR<sub>2</sub>) end up on the <b>sp<sup>2</sup></b> carbons of the product (the radical anion is protonated, and the carbanion forms, at the positions least destabilised: <i>ortho/meta</i> to the donor). Electron-<i>withdrawing</i> substituents (COOH, CONH<sub>2</sub>, CN) end up on the <b>sp<sup>3</sup></b> carbon (para protonation, carbanion stabilised by the acceptor). Anisole → 1-methoxycyclohexa-1,4-diene (hydrolysis gives the β,γ-unsaturated ketone, then the conjugated enone: a route to cyclohexenones); benzoic acid → cyclohexa-2,5-diene-1-carboxylic acid; toluene → 1-methylcyclohexa-1,4-diene.",
          "<b>Why an alcohol is needed:</b> NH<sub>3</sub> (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 38) is too weak an acid to protonate the benzene radical anion at a useful rate; ROH (≈ 16–18) does so. The first protonation is rate-determining. The 1,4-diene (not the conjugated 1,3-diene) forms because the pentadienyl anion is protonated at its central carbon, where the charge density (and the kinetic basicity) is highest.",
          "<b>Alkynes → trans-alkenes (Na/NH<sub>3</sub>).</b> The vinyl radical anion (intermediate) is protonated to a vinyl radical that adopts the less hindered trans geometry before a second electron gives the trans-vinyl anion, protonated to the trans-alkene; terminal alkynes are deprotonated first (acetylide) and are not reduced. Lindlar (H<sub>2</sub>, Pd/CaCO<sub>3</sub>, quinoline) gives the cis-alkene by syn addition.",
          "<b>Bouveault–Blanc</b> (ester + Na/EtOH → two alcohols) via ketyl and carbanion stages; replaced by LiAlH<sub>4</sub> today. <b>Acyloin condensation</b> (diester + Na, toluene, then TMSCl) closes rings from C<sub>5</sub> to C<sub>30</sub> through a diradical dianion on the metal surface (high dilution is not needed because the sodium surface holds both ends). <b>McMurry coupling</b> (2 R<sub>2</sub>C=O + TiCl<sub>3</sub>/LiAlH<sub>4</sub> or Zn–Cu → R<sub>2</sub>C=CR<sub>2</sub>): ketyl radical anions on low-valent titanium couple to a pinacolate and deoxygenate (Ti–O strength); used for tetrasubstituted alkenes and macrocycles. <b>SmI<sub>2</sub> (Kagan)</b> (THF/HMPA) reduces ketones, halides, and promotes ketyl–olefin cyclisations (a ketyl adds to an alkene in 5-exo mode).",
          "<b>Benkeser reduction</b> (Li/ethylamine): further reduces arenes to cyclohexenes. <b>Clemmensen</b> (Zn(Hg)/HCl) and <b>Wolff–Kishner</b> are not Birch-type; Clemmensen proceeds by zinc-carbenoid surface species.",
          "<b>Pinacol coupling:</b> 2 R<sub>2</sub>C=O + Mg(Hg)/or Al/or SmI<sub>2</sub> → vicinal diol. Intramolecular versions give cis-1,2-diols in rings (1,5- and 1,6-dicarbonyls give 5-/6-membered rings with cis selectivity because the metal chelates both oxygens). The acid-catalysed pinacol–pinacolone rearrangement of the product is a carbocation 1,2-shift (Part 7).",
          "<b>Nitroarene reduction steps</b> (one-electron stages): ArNO<sub>2</sub> → ArNO<sub>2</sub><sup>•−</sup> → ArNO → ArNHOH → ArNH<sub>2</sub>; with Zn/NH<sub>4</sub>Cl stops at hydroxylamine; in alkali, bimolecular products (azoxy, azo, hydrazo) form by coupling of nitroso and hydroxylamine intermediates (Part 14 table)."])
    B.h2("13.3  Radical cations: mass spectrometry, anodic chemistry, photoredox")
    B.ul(["<b>Electron-impact ionisation (70 eV)</b> removes the electron of highest energy: n(O), n(N) lone pairs &gt; π &gt; σ. The molecular ion M<sup>•+</sup> then fragments by pathways that give the most stable cation plus a neutral radical (or a neutral molecule plus a radical cation). Rules: (i) <b>nitrogen rule</b>: an odd number of N gives an odd M<sup>•+</sup> mass; (ii) <b>α-cleavage</b> next to O, N or C=O (radical on the heteroatom pairs with an α C–C electron): CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub><sup>•+</sup> → CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sup>+</sup> (m/z 43, loss of the larger radical is favoured) and C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CO<sup>+</sup> (57); (iii) <b>McLafferty rearrangement</b> of carbonyls with a γ-H: six-membered TS 1,5-H transfer to O and β-cleavage gives an enol radical cation and an alkene (e.g. butanal m/z 44; ketone/ester/acid/amide); (iv) <b>benzylic cleavage</b> to the tropylium ion (m/z 91) from alkylbenzenes; (v) <b>loss of small neutral molecules</b>: H<sub>2</sub>O (M − 18) from alcohols, CO (28), CO<sub>2</sub> (44), HCN (27), C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub> (28, retro-Diels–Alder of cyclohexenes), HX; (vi) isotopic patterns: Cl (3 : 1 for M : M+2), Br (1 : 1).",
          "<b>Anodic oxidation (electro-organic).</b> Kolbe (carboxylates, Part 9), Shono oxidation (carbamate/amide → N-acyliminium via α-C–H, then nucleophile trapping), oxidation of enol ethers, aromatic ethers (anisole → quinone ketals in MeOH). The radical cation of an electron-rich arene reacts with nucleophiles at the position of highest spin/charge density (e.g. para to OMe).",
          "<b>Chemical oxidants:</b> CAN (Ce<sup>4+</sup>), DDQ (hydride/SET abstraction from benzylic C–H of electron-rich arenes, deprotection of PMB ethers: ArCH<sub>2</sub>OR → ArCH=O<sup>+</sup>R → ArCHO + ROH), Fe(III)/Cu(II) in Scholl coupling, NO<sup>+</sup>BF<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup>, Wurster-type amine radical cations. <b>Photoredox catalysis</b> ([Ru(bpy)<sub>3</sub>]<sup>2+*</sup>, Ir(ppy)<sub>3</sub>, organic dyes; Nobel-adjacent field) uses visible light to generate radical anions/cations by SET: reductive quenching gives a radical anion of the substrate, oxidative quenching a radical cation; applications include radical dehalogenation, amine α-functionalisation, alkene hydrofunctionalisation, [2+2] cycloadditions via radical cation chains.",
          "<b>Radical-cation chain reactions.</b> The Bauld cation-radical Diels–Alder: an electron-rich dienophile radical cation (e.g. from trans-anethole with a triarylaminium salt) adds to a diene much faster than the neutral pair; the new radical cation oxidises another neutral dienophile (chain). Rate acceleration &gt; 10<sup>6</sup> and complementary regiochemistry.",
          "<b>Amine radical cations:</b> loss of α-H<sup>+</sup> gives α-aminoalkyl radicals; loss of an electron again gives iminium ions (the basis of photoredox α-functionalisation of tertiary amines and of the biological oxidation of N-methyl amines by cytochrome P450 to formaldehyde + dealkylated amine)."])
    B.h2("13.4  Radical anions in substitution: S<sub>RN</sub>1 and electron-transfer mechanisms")
    B.ul(["<b>S<sub>RN</sub>1 (Bunnett, 1970):</b> ArX + e<sup>−</sup> → ArX<sup>•−</sup> → Ar• + X<sup>−</sup> (initiation by hν, Na/NH<sub>3</sub>, electrochemistry, or Fe<sup>2+</sup>); Ar• + Nu<sup>−</sup> → ArNu<sup>•−</sup>; ArNu<sup>•−</sup> + ArX → ArNu + ArX<sup>•−</sup> (chain). Features: no EWG needed; leaving-group order I &gt; Br &gt; Cl ≫ F (opposite to S<sub>N</sub>Ar); nucleophiles are enolates, thiolates, amide, phosphide, acetone enolate; inhibited by radical traps and O<sub>2</sub> and by radical-anion scavengers (p-dinitrobenzene); promoted by light and by electron-transfer initiators; no cine substitution (distinguishes it from benzyne). The nucleophile enters where the leaving group was (ipso).",
          "<b>Single-electron transfer (SET) in organometallic reactions:</b> the formation of Grignard reagents (RX + Mg surface: RX<sup>•−</sup> → R• + X<sup>−</sup>), the reaction of RLi/RMgX with hindered ketones (benzophenone gives the deep-blue/purple ketyl observed by ESR), the Wurtz reaction (Na + RX), the Ullmann coupling. The SET versus polar-mechanism question is generally settled by radical probes: cyclopropylcarbinyl and 5-hexenyl halides give ring-opened/cyclised products if a free radical forms (Part 9).",
          "<b>Radical anions of alkyl halides</b> have a three-electron σ*(C–X) bond that dissociates in &lt; 10<sup>−12</sup> s (concerted dissociative ET), giving R• + X<sup>−</sup> directly."])
    B.warn("<b>Typical errors in radical-ion questions.</b> (1) Placing the Birch product double bonds in conjugation: they are 1,4 (skipped). (2) Putting EWG on sp<sup>2</sup> carbon and EDG on sp<sup>3</sup>: it is the opposite. (3) Using NH<sub>3</sub> alone as the proton source without an alcohol. (4) Reducing a terminal alkyne with Na/NH<sub>3</sub>: it is deprotonated first. (5) Assuming that the ionisation in MS removes an σ electron: it removes the highest-energy lone-pair/π electron. (6) Forgetting the nitrogen rule. (7) Treating S<sub>RN</sub>1 as S<sub>N</sub>Ar.")


def mechanisms(B):
    c = []
    c.append(ex("13.1", "Pinacol coupling: two ketyl radical anions (from acetone + Mg) combine at carbon",
        "[CH3:1][C:2]([CH3:3])[O-:4].[CH3:11][C:12]([CH3:13])[O-:14]",
        [St([R(('rad', 2), ('nb', 2, 12), 0.4), R(('rad', 12), ('nb', 2, 12), -0.4)], "radical coupling", rad=True, expect="CC(C)([O-])C(C)(C)[O-]")],
        "CC(C)([O-])C(C)(C)[O-]",
        "Each ketyl carries the unpaired electron on carbon (the spin is largely on C, the charge on O). Two ketyls pair their electrons to form the new C–C σ bond; the dialkoxide is protonated on work-up to the 1,2-diol (pinacol), or held by Mg<sup>2+</sup> as a five-membered chelate.", width=680))
    c.append(ex("13.2", "Mass spectrum of butan-2-one: α-cleavage of the radical cation (m/z 43)",
        "[CH3:1][C:2](=[O+:3])[CH2:4][CH3:5]",
        [St([R(('rad', 3), ('nb', 2, 3), 0.4), R(('b', 4, 2, 4), ('nb', 2, 3), -0.4)], "α-cleavage", rad=True, expect="CC#[O+].C[CH2]")],
        "CC#[O+].C[CH2]",
        "The radical on oxygen pairs with one electron of the adjacent C–C σ bond to form the third bond of the acylium ion CH<sub>3</sub>–C≡O<sup>+</sup> (m/z 43); the other σ electron stays on the departing ethyl radical, which is lost as a neutral. Cleavage that expels the larger alkyl radical is preferred, so m/z 43 is the base peak of butanone (m/z 57 is smaller).", width=680))
    B.grid(c, 2)


def problems(B, q):
    q(5, "Predict the Birch product from (a) toluene, (b) p-xylene, (c) benzoic acid, (d) anisole, all with Na/NH<sub>3</sub>(l)/EtOH.",
      S([("a", "CC1=CCC=CC1"), ("b", "CC1=CCC(C)=CC1"), ("c", "OC(=O)C1C=CCC=C1"), ("d", "COC1=CCC=CC1")]),
      "(a) 1-methylcyclohexa-1,4-diene; (b) 1,4-dimethylcyclohexa-1,4-diene; (c) cyclohexa-2,5-diene-1-carboxylic acid; (d) 1-methoxycyclohexa-1,4-diene",
      "Donor groups (CH<sub>3</sub>, OMe) stay on the double bonds; the acceptor (COOH) sits on the sp<sup>3</sup> carbon, with protonation at the ipso and para carbons. The two products of the second (para-dialkyl) case are the 1,4-diene with both methyls on sp<sup>2</sup> carbons.")
    q(6, "Complete: (a) hex-3-yne + Na/NH<sub>3</sub>(l); (b) hex-3-yne + H<sub>2</sub>, Lindlar; (c) hex-1-yne + Na/NH<sub>3</sub>(l) (1 equiv Na).",
      S([("a", "CC/C=C/CC"), ("b", "CC/C=C\\CC"), ("c", "CCCCC#C")]), "(a) (E)-hex-3-ene; (b) (Z)-hex-3-ene; (c) the terminal alkyne is deprotonated, giving sodium hex-1-ynide (not reduced)",
      "Dissolving-metal reduction proceeds through a trans-configured vinyl radical/anion; Lindlar hydrogenation is syn. A terminal C–H (pK<sub>a</sub> 25) is removed by the amide/NH<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup> formed in solution before reduction.")
    q(7, "(Integer) How many of the following form a radical anion on treatment with sodium metal in THF: naphthalene, benzophenone, cyclohexane, anthracene, acetone, tert-butyl alcohol?",
      None, "4  (naphthalene, benzophenone, anthracene, acetone)", "Sodium transfers an electron into a low-lying π* orbital: arenes with extended π systems and ketones form radical anions (naphthalenide green, benzophenone ketyl blue/purple, anthracenide green; acetone mainly pinacolises). Cyclohexane has no low-energy acceptor orbital; t-BuOH is deprotonated (H<sub>2</sub> evolution).")
    q(8, "A ketyl radical anion from benzophenone is generated with Na in dry THF. (a) What is its colour and what is it used for? (b) What happens on adding water?",
      None, "(a) deep blue/purple; indicator of dryness and O<sub>2</sub>-free conditions in solvent stills; (b) the colour is discharged and benzhydrol/benzopinacol form", "The persistent ketyl is destroyed by traces of water or O<sub>2</sub>, so its colour signals that the THF is dry. Protonation gives Ph<sub>2</sub>C•–OH → benzhydrol by H-atom pickup or benzopinacol by dimerisation.")
    q(9, "Arrange the following in decreasing order of intensity of the M<sup>•+</sup> peak in EI-MS: benzene, hexane, 2-methylpentan-2-ol, ethanol.",
      None, "benzene &gt; hexane &gt; ethanol &gt; 2-methylpentan-2-ol", "Aromatic molecular ions are the most stable (charge delocalised); linear alkanes give weak M<sup>•+</sup>, primary alcohols weaker still (rapid loss of H<sub>2</sub>O and α-cleavage), and tertiary alcohols are almost absent (very fast α-cleavage and dehydration).")
    q(10, "In the EI mass spectrum of 2-pentanone (C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O), the base peak is m/z 43 and there is a significant peak at m/z 58. Account for both.",
      S([("", "CCCC(C)=O")]), "m/z 43: CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sup>+</sup> by α-cleavage; m/z 58: McLafferty rearrangement (loss of ethene)", "α-Cleavage on the propyl side expels •C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>7</sub> to give CH<sub>3</sub>C≡O<sup>+</sup> (43). The γ-H (on C-5) is transferred to O through a six-membered transition state with cleavage of the α,β C–C bond, giving CH<sub>2</sub>=C(OH)CH<sub>3</sub><sup>•+</sup> (58) and neutral C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>.")
    q(11, "A chloro compound shows M<sup>•+</sup> peaks at m/z 78 and 80 in a 3 : 1 ratio, and an odd/even pattern that fits C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>7</sub>Cl. Identify the isotope pattern and give one fragmentation.",
      None, "one chlorine (<sup>35</sup>Cl : <sup>37</sup>Cl ≈ 3 : 1); loss of Cl• gives C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>7</sub><sup>+</sup> (m/z 43)", "The 3 : 1 intensity ratio of M and M+2 is the signature of one Cl atom (Br gives 1 : 1). C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>7</sub>Cl has M = 78 (<sup>35</sup>Cl) and 80 (<sup>37</sup>Cl); C–Cl cleavage gives the propyl cation, m/z 43.")
    q(12, "Why does S<sub>RN</sub>1 show leaving-group order I &gt; Br &gt; Cl ≫ F, opposite to S<sub>N</sub>Ar, and why is there no cine substitution?",
      None, "the rate-limiting step is C–X cleavage of the radical anion, not nucleophilic addition; no aryne is involved", "In S<sub>N</sub>Ar the addition (needing a polarised C–X, i.e. F) is rate-limiting. In S<sub>RN</sub>1 the fragmentation of ArX<sup>•−</sup> depends on C–X bond strength, favouring I and Br. The aryl radical couples with Nu<sup>−</sup> at the carbon that carried X, so the product is ipso (no cine).")
    q(13, "Acetone enolate reacts with iodobenzene under photostimulation in liquid ammonia to give phenylacetone; no product forms in the dark or in the presence of di-tert-butyl nitroxide. What is the mechanism?",
      None, "S<sub>RN</sub>1 radical chain", "Light supplies the initial electron transfer; phenyl radical (from PhI<sup>•−</sup>) couples with the enolate to give the radical anion of phenylacetone, which transfers an electron to PhI to propagate the chain. Radical scavengers (nitroxide) terminate the chain; the dark reaction does not initiate.")
    q(14, "Give the products of (a) acetophenone + SmI<sub>2</sub>/THF (ketyl coupling) and (b) benzaldehyde + TiCl<sub>3</sub>/Zn–Cu (McMurry).",
      S([("b", "c1ccccc1/C=C/c1ccccc1")]), "(a) 2,3-diphenylbutane-2,3-diol (pinacol); (b) (E)-stilbene", "(a) ketyl radical anions couple at carbon. (b) Low-valent titanium couples the ketyls to a titanium pinacolate and then deoxygenates it (Ti–O bond formation), giving mainly trans-stilbene.")
